id: design.leader-election
kind: design
title: Leader election
aliases: leader election, single active instance, failover
tags: distributed systems, coordination, availability
questions:
  - how would you make sure only one replica does a periodic job?
invariant: Exactly one instance holds leadership at a time, leadership is renewed through a lease, and losing the lease stops the leader's work.
answer_30s: Elect one leader through a lease in a consensus store, have it renew and step down on loss, and make every leader action safe to repeat under a second leader.
answer_2m: |
  In-process options first, because they are simpler and often sufficient: a
  Kubernetes singleton Deployment with replicas 1, a CronJob with
  concurrencyPolicy Forbid, or a work assignment table that grants each unit to
  one owner with a lease. Cross-process election is needed when you want
  high availability of the leader role, and then the mechanism is a
  lease-based election in a consensus system. The invariant that makes it safe
  is the same as locking: two leaders can briefly coexist (the previous one
  has not noticed the loss yet), so every leader action must be idempotent or
  guarded by a fencing token. Alert on leadership churn and on "no leader",
  because both are silent in normal dashboards.
when_to_use:
  - periodic cluster-wide jobs, cache warmers, single-writer reconciliation
avoid_when:
  - work that can be partitioned across replicas (prefer partition and scale instead)
commands:
  - kubectl get leases -n kube-system => the same lease mechanism Kubernetes itself uses for controller leadership
failure_modes:
  - a leader that keeps working after losing its lease (duplicate side effects)
  - election on a cache with no consensus, so partitions produce two leaders
  - no alert on leadership churn, so repeated failover goes unnoticed
related:
  - design.distributed-locking
  - design.idempotency
  - k8s.cron-job
  - k8s.reconciliation
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/leases/
  - https://etcd.io/docs/latest/learning/api/#lease-api
